This small example stores and updates one total.
```javascript
const counts = new Map();
counts.set("paper", 2);
counts.set("paper", counts.get("paper") + 3);
console.log(counts.get("paper"));
console.log(counts.size);
```
It prints 5 and then 1: two updates still describe one distinct item.

Here is a complete grouping example with a different input shape.
```javascript
const names = ["pen", "tape", "pen"];
const counts = new Map();
for (const name of names) {
  counts.set(name, (counts.get(name) ?? 0) + 1);
}
console.log([...counts]);
```
The entries are ["pen", 2] and ["tape", 1], in that order. In your project, add each row's quantity instead of always adding one.

