This example updates the same item twice.
```javascript
const counts = new Map();
counts.set("paper", 2);
counts.set("paper", counts.get("paper") + 3);
console.log(counts.get("paper"));
console.log(counts.size);
```
Output:
```text
5
1
```
The paper total is 5. `size` is 1 because the Map still has just one item.

This example counts how often each name appears.
```javascript
const names = ["pen", "tape", "pen"];
const counts = new Map();
for (const name of names) {
  counts.set(name, (counts.get(name) ?? 0) + 1);
}
console.log([...counts]);
```
The Map holds `"pen"` with 2, then `"tape"` with 1. `[...counts]` turns its entries into an array for display.

Your project follows the same pattern, but adds each row's `quantity` instead of 1.
